== Chapter XVI

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[The strength of Indwelling-sin, manifested from its power and effects in persons unregenerate.]
]

#smallcaps[IT] is of the power and efficacy of #emph[Indwelling-sin,] as it remains in several degrees in Believers that we are treating. Now I have else-where showed, that the nature and all the natural properties of it do still remain in them. Though therefore we cannot prove directly what is the #emph[strength] of sin in them, from what its #emph[power] is in those, in whom it is only checked and not at all weakened; yet may we from an observation thereof caution Believers of the real power of that #emph[mortal enemy] with whom they have to do.

If the Plague do violently rage in #emph[one] City, destroying multitudes, and there be in #emph[another] an infection of the same kind, which yet arises not unto that height and fury there, by reason of the #emph[correction] that it meets withal from a #emph[better air,] and remedies used; yet a man may demonstrate unto the Inhabitants the force and danger of that #emph[Infection] got in among them, by the #emph[effects] that it hath and doth produce among others, who have not the benefit of the #emph[preventives] and preservatives which they enjoy; which will both teach them to value the means of their preservation, and be the more watchful against the power of the infection that is among them. It is so in this case. Believers may be taught what is the power and efficacy of that #emph[plague of sin,] which is in and among them, by the #emph[effects] the same plague produceth in and among others, who have not those #emph[corrections] of its poison, and those #emph[preservatives] from death which the Lord Jesus hath furnished them withal.

Having then fixed on the Demonstration of the power of sin, from the #emph[effects] it doth produce, and having given a double instance hereof in #emph[Believers] themselves, I shall now farther evidence the same truth, or pursue the same evidence of it, by showing somewhat of the power that it acteth in them who are #emph[unregenerate,] and so have not the remedies against it which Believers are furnished withal.

I shall not handle the #emph[whole power of sin] in unregenerate persons, which is a very large field, and not the business I have in hand; but only by some #emph[few instances] of its effects in them, intimate, as I said, unto Believers, what they have to deal withal.

#emph[First] then, it appears in the #emph[violence] it offers to the nature of man, compelling them unto sins, fully contrary to all the principles of the reasonable nature wherewith they are endued from God. Every creature of God hath in its creation, a #emph[Law of operation] implanted in it, which is the rule of all that proceedeth from it, of all that it doth of its own accord. So the fire ascends upwards, bodies that are weighty and heavy descend; the water flows, each according to the Principles of their nature, which give them the #emph[Law of their Operation.] That which hinders them in their operation is force and violence, as that which hinders a #emph[stone] from descending, or the #emph[fire] from going upwards. That which forceth them to move contrary to the Law of their nature, as a stone to go upwards, or the fire to descend, is in its kind the greatest #emph[violence,] of which the degrees are endless. Now that which should take a great #emph[Millstone,] and fling it upwards into the air, all would acknowledge to be a matter of wonderful force, power, and efficacy.

Man also hath his #emph[Law of Operation] and working concreated with him. And this may be considered two ways; either, first, as it is #emph[common] to him with other creatures; or as #emph[peculiar,] with reference unto that special end for which he was made. Some things are, I say, in this #emph[Law of Nature common to man with other creatures;] as to nourish their young, to live quietly with them of the same kind and race with them; to seek and follow after that which is good for them in that state and condition wherein they are created. These are things which all #emph[brute living creatures] have in the Law of their nature, as Man also hath.

But now besides these things, man being created in an #emph[especial manner] to give Glory to God by rational and moral obedience, and so to obtain a reward in the enjoyment of him; there are many things in the #emph[Law of his creation] that are peculiar to him; as to #emph[love God above all,] to seek the enjoyment of him as his chiefest Good and last End, to inquire after his Mind and Will, and to yield obedience, and the like. All which are part of the Law of his Nature.

Now these things are not distinguished so, as though a man might perform the actions of the Law of his nature which are common to him with other creatures, merely from the #emph[principles of his nature] as they do; but the #emph[Law of his dependence upon God,] and doing all things in obedience unto him, passeth on them all also. He can never be considered as a #emph[mere creature,] but as a creature made for the glory of God, by rational moral obedience; #emph[rational,] because by him chosen, and performed with reason; and #emph[moral,] because regulated by a #emph[Law] whereunto Reason doth attend.

For instance; It is common to man with other creatures, to #emph[take care for the nourishing of his children,] of the young helpless ones that receive their being by him. There is implanted in him, in the principles of his nature concreated with them, a love and care for them; so is it with other living creatures. Now let other creatures answer this #emph[instinct and inclination,] and be not hardened against them like the #emph[foolish Ostrich,] unto whom God hath not imparted this natural wisdom, Job 39:16, 17. They fully answer the #emph[Law of their Creation.] With Man it is not so; it is not enough for him to answer the #emph[instinct] and secret impulse and inclination of his nature and kind, as in the nourishing of his children; but he must do it also in #emph[subjection to God,] and obey him therein, and doing it unto his glory; the Law of #emph[Moral Obedience] passing over all his whole being and all his operations; but in these things lie, as it were, the #emph[whole] of a man, namely in the things which are implanted in his nature as a creature, common to him with all other living creatures, seconded by the Command or Will of God, as he is a creature capable of #emph[yielding moral obedience,] and doing all things for his glory.

That then, which shall drive and compel a man to transgress this #emph[Law of his nature,] which is not only as to #emph[throw millstones upward,] to drive Beasts from taking care of their young, to take from Cattle of the same kind the #emph[herding of themselves] in quietness, but moreover, to cast off what lies in him his #emph[fundamental dependence on God,] as a creature made to yield him obedience, must needs be esteemed of great force and efficacy.

Now this is frequently done by #emph[Indwelling-sin,] in persons #emph[unregenerate.] Let us take some few instances.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, There is nothing that is more deeply inlaid in the principles of the natures of all living creatures, and so of man himself, than a love unto, and a care for the preservation and nourishing of their young: many brute creatures will #emph[die] for them, some feed them with their own flesh and blood; all deprive themselves of that #emph[food] which nature directs them to as their best, to #emph[impart] it to them; and acting in their behalf to the utmost of their power.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Now such is the efficacy, power, and force of #emph[Indwelling-sin] in man, an #emph[infection] that the nature of other creatures knows nothing of, that in many it prevails to stop this fountain, to beat back the stream of natural affections, to root up the principles of the Law of nature, and to drive them unto a #emph[neglect,] a destruction of the #emph[fruit of their own loins. Paul] tells us of the old Gentiles, they were ἄστοργοι, Rom 1:31. #emph[without natural affection;] that which he aims at is that barbarous custom among the #emph[Romans,] who oft-times to spare the trouble in the education of their children, and to be at liberty to satisfy their lusts, #emph[destroyed their own children from the womb.] So far did the strength of sin prevail to obliterate the Law of nature, and to repel the force and power of it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Examples of this nature are common in all Nations; amongst ourselves, of #emph[women murdering their own children,] through the deceitful reasoning of sin. And herein sin turns the strong #emph[current] of nature, darkens all the #emph[light] of God in the soul, controls all natural #emph[principles,] influenced with the power of the command and will of God. But yet this #emph[evil] hath, through the efficacy of sin, received a fearful aggravation. Men have not only #emph[slain,] but cruelly #emph[sacrificed] their children to satisfy their lusts. The Apostle reckons #emph[Idolatry,] and so consequently all #emph[superstition,] among the works of the flesh, Gal 5:20. That is, the fruit and product of #emph[indwelling sin.] Now from hence it is that men have offered that horrid and unspeakable violence to the Law of Nature mentioned. So the Psalmist tells us, Ps 106:37, 38. The same is again mentioned, Jer 7:31, and in sundry other places. The whole manner of that abomination I have elsewhere declared. For the present it may suffice to intimate, that they took their children and #emph[burnt them to ashes] in a soft fire; the wicked #emph[Priests] that assisted in the sacrifice affording them this relief, that they made a noise and clamor, that the vile wretches might not hear the woeful moans and cries of the #emph[poor dying tormented Infants.] I suppose in this case we need no farther evidence. #emph[Naturalists] can give no rational account, they can only admire the secret force of that #emph[little Fish,] which, they say, will stop a ship in full sail in the midst of the Sea. And we must acknowledge, that it is beyond our power to give an account of that #emph[secret force,] and unsearchable deceit that is in this #emph[inbred] Traitor, Sin; that cannot only stop the course of nature, when all the #emph[sails] of it that carry it forward are so filled, as they are in that of affections to children; but also #emph[drive it backward] with such a violence and force, as to cause men so to deal with their #emph[own children,] as a good man would not be hired with any reward to deal with his #emph[dog.] And it may not be to the disadvantage of the #emph[best,] to know and consider, that they carry that about them, in them, which in others hath produced these effects.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The like may be spoken of all other sins against the #emph[prime dictates] of the Law of Nature, that mankind is or hath been stained and defamed withal. #emph[Murder] of #emph[Parents] and Children, of Wives and Husbands, #emph[Sodomy, Incest,] and the like enormities; in all which, sin prevails in men against the #emph[whole Law of their Being] and dependence upon God.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[What should I reckon up the murders of #emph[Cain] and #emph[Abel,] the treason of #emph[Judas,] with their aggravations; or remind the filth and villany of #emph[Nero,] in whom sin seemed to design an instance of what it could #emph[debase the nature] of man unto: In a word, all the studied, premeditated #emph[perjuries;] all the designed, bloody #emph[revenges;] all the filth and uncleanness; all the enmity to God and his ways that is in the world, is fruit growing from this root alone.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, It evidences its efficacy in keeping men off from #emph[believing] under the dispensation of the Gospel. This evidence must be a little further cleared.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, Under the dispensation of the Gospel, there are #emph[but few that do believe.] So the Preachers of it complain, Isa 53:1. #emph[Who hath believed our report?] which the Apostle interprets of the #emph[paucity] of Believers, John 12:38. Our Savior Christ himself tells us, that #emph[many are called,] the Word is preached unto many, but #emph[few are chosen.] And so the Church complains of its number, Mic 7:1. #emph[Few] there be who enter the #emph[narrow gate,] daily experience confirms this woeful observation. How many Villages, Parishes, yea, Towns may we go unto, where the Gospel, it may be, hath been preached many years, and perhaps scarce meet a #emph[true Believer] in them, and one who shows forth the death of Christ in his conversation. In the best places, and most eminent for profession, are not such persons like the #emph[berries after the shaking of an Olive-tree,] two or three in the top of the uppermost boughs, and 4 or 5 in the highest branches?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, There is proposed to men in the preaching of the Gospel, as #emph[motives unto Believing] every thing in conjunction, that #emph[severally] prevail with men to do whatever else they do in their lives. Whatever any one doth with consideration, he doth it either because it is #emph[reasonable] and #emph[good] for him so to do, or #emph[profitable] and advantageous, or #emph[pleasant,] or lastly, #emph[necessary] for the avoidance of evil; whatever, I say, men do with consideration, whether it be good or evil, whether it be in the works of this life, or in things that lead to another; they do it from one or other of the reasons or motives mentioned. And, God knows, oft-times they are very #emph[poor] and mean in their kind, that men are prevailed upon by. How often will men for a very #emph[little pleasure,] a very #emph[little profit,] be induced to do that which shall embitter their lives, and damn their souls. And what industry will they use to avoid that which they apprehend #emph[evil] or grievous to them. And any one of these is enough to #emph[oil the wheels] of men's utmost endeavors, and set men at work to the purpose.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But now all these things center in the proposal of the Gospel, and the #emph[command of Believing;] and every one of them #emph[in a kind,] that the whole world can propose nothing like unto it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[It is the most #emph[reasonable thing] that can be proposed to the understanding of a man; that he who through his #emph[own default] hath lost that #emph[way] of bringing glory to God, and saving his own soul, (for which ends he was made) that he was first placed in, should accept of, and embrace, that other, blessed, easy, safe, excellent #emph[way,] for the attaining the ends mentioned, which God in infinite Grace, Love, Mercy, Wisdom, and Righteousness hath found out, and doth propose unto him. And,]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Secondly,] It is the #emph[profitablest] thing that a man can possibly be invited unto; if there be any #emph[profit,] or benefit, any advantage in the #emph[forgiveness of sins,] in the love and favor of God, in a blessed immortality, in eternal glory. And,]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Thirdly,] It is most #emph[pleasant] also; surely it is a pleasant thing to be brought out of #emph[darkness into light,] out of a dungeon unto a Throne, from captivity and slavery to Satan and cursed lusts, to the glorious liberty of the children of God, with a thousand heavenly #emph[sweetness] not now to be mentioned. And,]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Fourthly,] It is surely #emph[necessary,] and that not only from the #emph[command of God,] who hath the Supreme Authority over us, but also indispensably so for the avoidance of #emph[eternal ruin] of body and soul, Mark 16:16. It is constantly proposed under these terms, believe, or you perish under the weight of the wrath of the great God, and that for evermore.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But now notwithstanding that all these considerations are #emph[preached] unto men, and pressed upon them in the Name of the great God from day to day, from one year to another; yet, as was before observed, very #emph[few] there are who set their hearts unto them, so as to embrace that which they lead unto. Tell men #emph[ten thousand times,] that this is Wisdom, yea, Riches, that all their profit lies in it, that they will assuredly and eternally perish, and that it may be within a few hours, if they receive not the Gospel, assure them that it is their #emph[only interest] and concernment, let them know that God himself speaks all this unto them; yet all is one, they regard it not, set not their hearts unto it, but, as it were, plainly say, #emph[We will have nothing to do with these things;] they will rather perish in their lusts than accept of mercy.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, It is #emph[Indwelling-sin] that both disenableth men unto, and hinders them from believing, and that alone. #emph[Blindness] of mind, stubbornness of the will, sensuality of the affections, all concur to keep poor perishing souls at a distance from Christ. Men are made #emph[blind] by sin, and cannot see his Excellencies, #emph[obstinate] and will not lay hold of his Righteousness #emph[senseless] and take no notice of their own eternal concernments.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Now certainly that which can prevail with men wise and sober, and prudent in other things, to neglect and despise the love of God, the blood of Christ, the eternal welfare of their own souls, upon weak and worthless pretenses, must be acknowledged to have an astonishable force and efficacy accompanying it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Whose heart, who hath once heard of the ways of God, can but bleed to see #emph[poor souls] eternally perishing under a #emph[thousand gracious invitations,] to accept of mercy and pardon in the blood of Christ? And can we but be astonished at the power of that principle from whence it is, that they run headlong to their own destruction? And yet all this befalls them from the power and deceit of #emph[sin] that dwelleth in them.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. It is evident in their #emph[total Apostasies.] Many Men not really converted, are much wrought upon by the Word. The Apostle tells us, that they do #emph[clean escape them that live in Error,] as 2 Pet 2:18. They separate themselves from Idolatry and false Worship, owning and professing the Truth; and they also escape #emph[the pollution of the world,] v. 20. That is, the #emph[corruption that is in the world, through lust,] as he expresseth it, ch. 1:4, those filthy corrupt and unclean ways, which the Men of the world, in the pursuit of their lusts, do walk and live in; these they #emph[escape] from, in the amendment of their lives, and ordering of their Conversation according to the Convictions which they have from the Word. For so he tells us, that all this is brought about, #emph[through the knowledge of the Lord and Savior Jesus Christ;] that is, by the preaching of the Gospel; they are so far wrought upon, #emph[as to forsake] all ways of false Worship, to #emph[profess] the truth, to #emph[reform] their lives, and to walk answerable to the Convictions that are upon them.]

By this means do they gain the Reputation of Professors, #emph[They have a Name to live,] Rev 3:1, and are made partakers of some, or all of those #emph[privileges] of the Gospel, that are numbered by the Apostle, Heb 6:4, 5.

It is not my present business to show #emph[how far,] or wherein a Man may be #emph[effectually wrought] upon by the Word, and yet not be #emph[really wrought] over to close with Christ; or what may be the utmost bounds and limits of a #emph[common work] of grace upon unregenerate Men; It is on all hands confessed, that it may be carried on so far, that it is very difficult to discern between its effects and productions, and those of that Grace which is special and saving.

But now, notwithstanding all this, we see many of these daily #emph[fall off from God,] utterly and wickedly some into #emph[debauchery] and uncleanness, some to #emph[worldliness] and covetousness, some to be #emph[Persecutors] of the Saints, all to the perdition of their own souls. How this comes about, the Apostle declares in that place mentioned; they are, saith he, #emph[entangled] again; to #emph[entice and entangle] as I have showed before from James 1:14, 15, is the proper work of #emph[Indwelling Sin;] it is that alone which #emph[entangles] the Soul; as the Apostle speaks #emph[v.] 18. #emph[They are allured from their whole Profession,] into cursed Apostasy through the lusts of the flesh.

It prevails upon them through its deceit and power to an utter #emph[relinquishment of their Profession;] and their whole engagement unto God: And this several ways evinces the greatness of its strength and efficacy:

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. In that it giveth stop or control unto that #emph[exceeding greatness of power] which is put forth in the Word, in their Conviction and Reformation. We see it by experience, that Men are not #emph[easily wrought] upon by the Word; the most of men can live under the dispensation of it all the days of their lives, and continue as senseless and stupid as the #emph[seats] they sit upon, or the flint in the Rock of Stone. Mighty difficulties, and prejudices must be conquered, great #emph[strokes] must be given to the Conscience before this can be brought about. It is as the stopping of a #emph[River] in his course, and turning his streams another way; the hindering of a #emph[stone] in his falling downwards, or the turning away of the #emph[wild Ass,] when furiously set to pursue his way, as the Prophet speaks, Jer 2:24. To turn Men from their corrupt ways, sins and pleasures, to make them #emph[pray, fast, hear,] and do many things contrary to the Principle of flesh, which is secretly predominant in them, willingly and gladly; to cause them to #emph[profess Christ] and the Gospel, it may be under some trials and reproaches, to give them #emph[Light] to see into sundry mysteries, and #emph[gifts] for the discharge of sundry duties; to make #emph[dead,] blind, senseless Men, to #emph[walk,] and #emph[talk,] and do all the outward offices and duties of living and healthy Men; with the like attendances of Conviction and Reformation, are the effects and products of mighty power and strength. Indeed the power that the Holy Ghost puts forth by the word, in the staggering and #emph[conviction] of sinners, in the #emph[wakening] of their Consciences, the #emph[enlightening] of their minds, the #emph[changing] of their affections, the #emph[awing] of their hearts, the #emph[reforming] of their #emph[lives,] and #emph[compelling] them to duties, is inexpressible.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But now, unto all these is there #emph[check] and control given by #emph[Indwelling Sin.] It prevails against this #emph[whole work] of the Spirit by the Word, with all the advantages of Providential Dispensations, in Afflictions and Mercies, wherewith it is attended. When Sin is once enraged, all these things become but like the withes and cords wherewith #emph[Samson] was bound before his head was shaven: Cry but to it, the #emph[Philistines] are upon thee, here is a #emph[subtle, a suitable temptation,] now show thy strength and efficacy, all these things become like #emph[Tow that have smelt the fire. Conscience] is stifled, #emph[Reputation] in the Church of God despised, #emph[Light] supplanted, the #emph[impressions] of the Word cast off, #emph[convictions] digested, Heaven and Hell are despised; Sin makes its way through all, and utterly turns the Soul from the good and right ways of God. Sometimes it doth this subtly by #emph[imperceptible degrees,] taking off all force of former impressions from the Spirit, by the Word, sullying Conscience by degrees, hardening the Heart, and making sensual the Affections by various workings, that the poor #emph[backslider in heart] scarce knows what he is doing, until he be come to the very bottom of all impiety, profaneness, and enmity against God. Sometimes falling in conjunction with some #emph[vigorous temptation,] it suddenly, and at once plunges the Soul into a course of alienation from God, and the profession of his ways.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. It takes them off from those #emph[hopes of Heaven,] which upon their Convictions, Obedience, and #emph[temporary Faith] or believing, they had attained. There is a #emph[general hope of Heaven,] or at least of the escaping of Hell, of an #emph[untroublesome immortality] in the most sottish and stupid Souls in the World, who either by tradition or instruction from the Word, are persuaded that there is another state of things to come after this life; but it is in #emph[unconvinced,] unenlightened Persons, a dull, senseless, unaffecting thing, that hath no other hold upon them, nor power in them, but only to keep them free from the trouble and perplexity of contrary thoughts and apprehensions. The matter is otherwise with them who by the Word are so wrought upon as we have before declared; their #emph[hope of Heaven] and a blessed Immortality is oft-times accompanied with great #emph[joys,] and exultations, and is a relief unto them, under and against the worst of their fears and trials; It is such, as they would not #emph[part withal] for all the World; and upon all occasions they retreat in their minds unto it, for comfort and relief.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Now all this by the #emph[Power of Sin] are they prevailed withal to forgo. Let Heaven go if it will, a blessed Immortality with the enjoyment of God himself, Sin must be served, and provision made to fulfil the lusts thereof.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[If a Man, in the things of this World, had such a hope of a large #emph[Inheritance,] of a Kingdom, as wherein he is satisfied that it will not fail him, but that in the issue he shall surely enjoy it, and lead an happy and a glorious life in the possession of it many days; if one should go to him and tell him, It is true, the #emph[Kingdom] you look for is an ample and honorable Dominion, full of all good things desirable, and you may attain it; but come, cast away all hopes and expectations of it, and come join with Me in the #emph[service and slavery] of such or such an oppressing Tyrant. You will easily grant; he must have some strange bewitching power with him, that should prevail with a Man in his wits to follow his advice.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Yet thus it is, and much more so in the case we have in hand. Sin itself cannot deny, but that the #emph[Kingdom of Heaven] which the Soul is in hope and expectation of, is glorious and excellent, nor doth it go about to convince him, that his thoughts of it are #emph[vain,] and such as will deceive him, but plainly prevails with him to cast away his #emph[hopes,] to despise his #emph[Kingdom] that he was in expectation of, and that upon no other motive but that he may serve some #emph[worldly, cruel,] or filthy and sensual lust; Certainly here lies a secret efficacy, whose depths cannot be fathomed.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. The Apostle manifests the power of the entanglements of sin in and upon #emph[Apostates,] in that it turns them off from #emph[the way of righteousness after they have known it,] 2 Pet 2:21. It will be found at the last day #emph[an evil thing and a bitter,] that Men live all their days in the service of sin, self, and the World, refusing to make any #emph[trial] of the #emph[ways of God] whereunto they are invited; though they have no experience of their excellency, beauty, pleasantness, safety, yet having evidence brought unto them from God himself, that they are so, the refusal of them will, I say, be #emph[bitterness] in the latter end. But their condition is yet far worse, who as the Apostle speaks, having #emph[known the way of righteousness,] are by the power of #emph[Indwelling Sin] turned aside from the holy Commandment. To leave God for the Devil, after a Man hath made some trial of #emph[him] and his service; Heaven for Hell; after a Man hath had some cheering, refreshing thoughts of it, the fellowship of the Saints, for an #emph[Alehouse] or a #emph[brothel-house;] after a man hath been admitted unto their Communion, and tasted of the pleasantness of it, to leave walking in pure, clear, straight paths, to #emph[wallow in mire,] draughts and filth, this will be for a #emph[lamentation;] Yet this doth sin prevail upon #emph[Apostates] unto; and that against all their Light, Conviction, Experiences, Professions, Engagements, or whatever may be #emph[strong] upon them to keep them to the known ways of Righteousness.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. It evinces its strength in them by prevailing with them unto #emph[a total renunciation of God] as revealed in Christ, and the Power of all Gospel-Truth, #emph[in the Sin against the Holy Ghost.] I do not now precisely determine what is the #emph[Sin against the Holy Ghost;] nor wherein it doth consist. There are different apprehensions of it; all agree in this; that by it an end is put to #emph[all dealings between God and Man in a way of Grace.] It is a sin unto death. And this doth the hardness and blindness of many Men's hearts bring them to; they are by them at length set out of the reach of Mercy. #emph[They choose to have no more to do with God;] and God swears that they shall never enter into his Rest. So #emph[Sin brings forth death.] A Man by it, is brought to renounce the End for which he was made; #emph[wilfully] to reject the Means of his coming to the enjoyment of God, to provoke him to his face; and so to perish in his Rebellion.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I have not mentioned these things, as though I hoped by them to set out to the #emph[full the power of Indwelling Sin in unregenerate Men;] only by a few Instances I thought to give a glimpse of it. He that would have a fuller view of it, had need only to #emph[open his eyes,] to take a little view of that wickedness which #emph[reigneth,] yea #emph[rageth] all the World over. Let him consider the prevailing flood of the things mentioned by #emph[Paul] to be #emph[the fruits of the flesh,] Gal 5:19, 20, 21. That is, among the Sons of Men, in all places, Nations, Cities, Towns, Parishes; and then let him add thereunto but this one consideration, that the World, which is full of the steam, filth, and blood of these abominations, as to their outward actings of them, is a #emph[Pleasant Garden,] a #emph[Paradise,] compared to the heart of Man, wherein they are all conceived, and hourly #emph[millions] of more vile abominations, which being stifled in the womb, by some of the ways before insisted on, they are never able to bring forth to light. Let a Man I say using the Law for his Light and Rule, take this course, and if he have any spiritual discerning, he may quickly attain satisfaction in this matter.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And I showed in the entrance of this discourse, how this consideration doth fully confirm the Truth proposed.]]
